"""Updated narration scripts for the three original decks (v2 — synced to new slide text)."""

LOYALTY = [
    "Welcome to The Frictionless Restaurant Paradigm, a complete strategy, design, and implementation playbook for replacing passive restaurant marketing with an interactive QR loyalty hub. The central premise is simple: passive marketing is dying, and the restaurants that survive will be the ones that give customers something to do, not something to ignore.",
    "Every rectangular banner ad in a predictable position gets habitually ignored. The eye learns to skip it. But the moment a user must perform an action, scratch a card, spin a wheel, or tap a reveal, they shift from passive observer to active participant. That shift is measurable in recall and conversion. This is the interaction imperative, and it is the foundational design principle behind everything in this playbook.",
    "The app download funnel for independent restaurants is essentially broken. A customer scans a QR code, gets redirected to the App Store, waits for a download, registers an account, and abandons. That flow loses the majority of potential loyalty members before they ever enroll. The Wallet Express solves this by eliminating every step between the scan and the reward. User scans QR, lands on the web hub, adds a pass to Apple or Google Wallet. Total time: 10 seconds. Zero App Store commitment required.",
    "The QR card is not just a loyalty pass. It is a four-in-one digital hub. First, a Digital Name Card with Web Contact API integration that loads in under two seconds. Second, a Loyalty and Membership pass integrated directly into Apple and Google Wallet. Third, a Website Hub with single-scroll, above-fold priority navigation covering Specials, Book, and Menu. Fourth, a Communication Hub with opt-in push notifications and geofencing. Critically, all routing is governed by dynamic URLs, so the destination can be updated weekly without reprinting a single physical card.",
    "Not all interactive formats are created equal. At the top of the engagement depth hierarchy sit Rewarded Ads, where a scratch card reveals a coupon code, and Playable Ads, where a spin-to-win wheel runs as a mini-game experience. Both deliver high engagement depth. Below those are Interactive Banners for medium-high engagement, like menu exploration and dish reveals, and niche customization tools like a build-your-own-roll simulator. Brands report thousands of new loyalty enrollments simply by switching from passive banners to the top-tier gamified formats.",
    "Four UX design principles govern the hub experience. One: one focal action per section. Scroll is momentum, not action, and competing calls-to-action cancel each other out. Two: immediate feedback loops, like a toast notification confirming when a menu item is added. Three: micro-personalization, which means showing the customer their customized membership card before asking for the sign-up commitment, drastically reducing friction. Four: intentional motion only. No decorative animation. Elevation on hover signals interactivity without visual noise.",
    "Seventy percent of global consumers prefer loyalty integrated directly into their native wallet app rather than a standalone brand application. The Wallet Pass design has strict constraints that maximize that preference. The square logo must be a minimum of 160 by 160 pixels for proper rendering in Notification Center. The strip image is 375 by 144 pixels as a dynamic background banner. No more than three to four visible fields are shown: member name, points balance, and tier status. Every pass auto-generates a member-unique barcode or QR code for POS scanning.",
    "The three-tier loyalty structure provides a transparent progression ladder that customers can see and strive toward. Regular tier covers zero to 499 points at 10 points per dollar spent, rewarding members with a birthday offer and a standard digital card. Gold tier covers 500 to 1,999 points at 12 points per dollar, unlocking priority reservations, a monthly bonus, and a soft-touch laminate physical card. Sakura Elite begins at 2,000 points at 15 points per dollar, granting omakase priority, chef's table access, and a heavy metal physical card with an embedded NFC chip. Earn rates and thresholds must be printed directly on the wallet pass. Transparency is not optional.",
    "The psychology behind gamification is well-documented. It increases customer engagement by up to 47% and drives 30% higher retention. The mechanics map directly to two human motivations. The need for achievement, progress, and mastery is satisfied through tier progression, leaderboards, and streaks. The desire for tangible reward and the pleasure of surprise is satisfied through coupons, free items, spin-to-win anticipation, and scratch card surprise mechanics. Every game element in the system is tied to one of these two psychological drivers.",
    "The scratch card mechanic has four design requirements that make it work. First, authentic texture via HTML5 Canvas with destination-out compositing, which replicates the physical scratch feel. Second, auto-reveal triggers at 55 to 60% scratched to honor impatient users who would otherwise abandon. Third, the Zeigarnik Effect: progress indicators actively compel users to complete what they have started. Fourth and most important, the tease mechanic: show the shape of the reward before scratching begins, triggering dopaminergic anticipation before the reveal.",
    "The spin-to-win wheel combines randomness with agency, giving the user a sense of control over an uncertain outcome. The animation must use an ease-out quartic deceleration curve because realistic slowing builds peak anticipation at the moment the wheel stops. Prize distribution should follow a 1-to-2 high-value entree prizes, 3-to-4 mid-tier discounts, and 2-to-3 low-value point rewards structure, ensuring everyone wins something every time. Critically, there must be a strict limit of one spin per day per member. Scarcity creates the daily return habit.",
    "The end-to-end redemption flow runs six steps with zero manual reconciliation. Step one: customer scans the QR code from a table tent or physical card. Step two: they land on the hub page and play the game or claim their offer. Step three: the system generates a unique UTM and timestamp code. Step four: the cashier scans or enters the code at the POS terminal. Step five: the discount is applied and points are logged in the database. Step six: the wallet pass auto-updates, pushing a lock screen notification of the new balance. Dynamic code generation prevents screenshot sharing and enables exact attribution by table or physical placement.",
    "The physical card itself follows a strict two-sided design blueprint. The front face carries a maximum of five typographic elements: the SVG or vector logo, staff name and title, and the restaurant URL for desktop users who want to find the page manually. The back face features one large dynamic QR code, minimum 2.5 centimeters, with clear reinforcement text reading Scan for Menu, Loyalty, and Offers. Premium tiers receive heavy stock or metal cards with embedded NFC chips, enabling tap-to-hub dual functionality alongside the standard QR scan.",
    "The 16-week rollout is structured into three sequential phases. Phase one, weeks one through four, establishes the foundation: print dynamic QR cards, set up the hub landing page targeting under 1.5 seconds load time, create the Wallet Pass template, and train staff on the POS flow. Phase two, weeks five through eight, activates gamification: launch the scratch card welcome offer, add daily spin-to-win, configure three-tier push notifications, and implement a 300-meter geo-fenced push trigger. Phase three, weeks nine through sixteen, optimizes: analyze QR scan sources by placement, A/B test notification timing, launch a referral program, and introduce member leaderboards.",
    "The success metrics tell the complete story of a healthy loyalty ecosystem. Target a 40-plus percent QR scan rate among customers who receive a physical card. A 25-plus percent wallet pass install rate from initial scans. A 35-plus percent higher return visit rate among members versus non-members. 20-plus percent daily engagement from active pass holders spinning or scratching. A 15 to 25 percent push notification open rate delivered directly to the lock screen. And a greater than 30 percent points redemption rate, proving the system has real liquidity. No custom app. Zero friction. A complete customer engagement ecosystem.",
]

SPORTS = [
    "The Arenza Play-to-Earn Engagement Model fuses Sports FAST streaming, gamified prediction, and local commerce into a single closed-loop monetization flywheel. Picture two screens side by side. On the left, a television broadcasting live football, generating $11 CPM from passive viewers who are barely paying attention. On the right, the same broadcast on a smartphone, overlaid with an interactive prediction asking who scores next, generating verified engagement, attributable data, and a premium CPM. The difference between those two screens is the entire Arenza business model.",
    "Arenza is not creating a new behavior. It is monetizing a $100 billion plus behavioral convergence that already exists. Five macro markets are colliding at once. FAST advertising, projected at $31.8 billion by 2030. Second screen behavior, a $26.18 billion market where 80-plus percent of live sports fans already hold a smartphone while watching. Sports fan engagement, projected at $66.7 billion by 2034. Live commerce, integrating real-world purchases with live content. And zero-party data, now the only viable targeting asset in a post-cookie, post-ATT world. Arenza is the interactive canvas at the center of all five.",
    "Three structural failures are breaking the current CTV model, and each one has a direct engineered fix. Ad Fatigue: mid-roll completion rates have dropped from 82% to 57% and passive spots get ignored. The fix is opt-in, non-interruptive gamified panels. The Companion App Crisis: 70 to 80% of sports companion apps lose users within 30 days because they have no temporal hooks tied to the live broadcast. The fix is pre-game, in-game, and post-game mechanics that create appointment-based habits. The QR Misconception: BrightLine data shows a 0.004% TV-to-mobile scan rate because it requires high-friction cross-device bridging. The fix is removing the TV scan entirely and keeping the QR code inside the mobile wallet for direct POS presentation.",
    "The Arenza platform architecture is a four-layer stack. Layer one is video infrastructure: a low-latency Sports FAST stream with sub-500 millisecond end-to-end delivery via HLS and DASH, plus SSAI suppression to eliminate mid-roll interruptions. Layer two is the Interactive Prediction Engine: SGAI overlays surface context-aware prediction microgames triggered by real-time SCTE-35 broadcast cues. Layer three is Zero-Party Data Enrichment: on-device CoreML processes watch time, team preferences, and game interaction signals without transmitting any raw personal information off the device. Layer four is Local Commerce and POS Integration: HMAC-signed, single-use QR reward tokens with direct webhook integration to Toast, Square, and Clover POS systems.",
    "The play-to-earn user journey runs four steps. Step one, Trigger: an SCTE-35 broadcast cue fires a subtle bottom overlay during live play asking the user a prediction question. Step two, Engage: the user taps a choice, an opt-in, non-disruptive, skill-based prediction that takes seconds and never interrupts the video. Step three, Earn: points accumulate based on prediction accuracy and streak multipliers, building toward a reward threshold. Step four, Redeem: once the threshold is reached, a single-use QR code unlocks a real-world offer, for example Domino's 20% off, ready to scan directly at the POS counter.",
    "The gamification model and the profile targeting model are not alternatives. They are the exact same engine running in a virtuous cycle. Step one: the viewer engages with a prediction, generating a direct SGAI overlay CPM. Step two: the user earns points and redeems a sponsor coupon, generating a sponsor activation and commerce transaction fee. Step three: the prediction data feeds the on-device CoreML engine, revealing team loyalty and purchase intent. Step four: the enriched user segment profile triggers higher OpenRTB bid requests. Step five: the next ad break sells at a premium $55-plus average CPM. Higher revenue funds better rewards. The loop repeats and compounds with every game.",
    "Three independent industry data points confirm the market is ready for this model. Samsung Ads GameBreaks delivered a 53% lift in unaided brand recall versus standard video, with 89% viewer preference. Arenza's edge over Samsung: Samsung keeps games confined to the ad break. Arenza embeds them during live play. Monumental Sports Network became the first local rights holder to integrate live polls and predictions outside Las Vegas. StreamLayer and Genius Sports demonstrated 2 to 3 times higher session minutes via in-stream interactivity, with 76% longer tune-in for interactive viewers. Arenza's edge: linking that heightened attention directly to localized POS QR redemption.",
    "The local SMB advertising market has been completely locked out of premium sports inventory. A standard national CTV buy requires $50,000 or more, broad targeting, and delivers pure awareness with no attribution. The Arenza local buy changes every variable. Campaign entry starts at $200 to $500 via FAST programmatic infrastructure. Targeting defaults to zip-code level geo-targeting within a 5-mile radius using CoreLocation. The result is closed-loop offline attribution, connecting the FAST CTV impression to an in-wallet QR scan at the local sports bar's register, providing undeniable causal linkage from screen to sale. That is the holy grail of attribution for local advertisers.",
    "Two structural advantages compound over time. First, bypassing the ATT ceiling: passive profiling requires Apple ATT consent, which suffers a 25% opt-in drop-off, yielding optimized data from only 25% of the user base. Arenza's gamified prediction captures accurate, explicit zero-party data from 100% of engaged users because it is voluntarily provided as part of the game, completely sidestepping ATT restrictions. Second, solving the 30-day drop-off: APNs temporal push notifications transform random usage into habitual appointment viewing. Spins unlock one hour before kickoff, a bonus spin goes live for 60 seconds during the game, and reward expiry fires two hours after the final whistle.",
    "Arenza operates entirely within a 50-state legal safe harbor. Sports wagering platforms like DraftKings and FanDuel are legal in only 33 states and require state gaming licenses. Paid-entry daily fantasy sites like PrizePicks are legal in only 26 states. Prediction markets like Kalshi are under active CFTC litigation. Arenza's free-to-play plus sponsor rewards model is legal in all 50 states and requires zero licensing of any kind. This is achieved by maintaining three strict conditions without exception: no monetary entry fee, non-cash rewards only, and skill-based scoring throughout. Zero legal exposure.",
    "The competitive positioning matrix confirms the white space. Arenza is the only platform that checks all five boxes simultaneously: Sports FAST video, interactive games, local SMB targeting, QR attribution, and offline POS redemption. Roku Ads Manager has partial QR attribution but no offline redemption and no local SMB targeting. StreamLayer has interactive games but serves enterprise clients only, with no local targeting or POS redemption. BrightLine and Genius BetVision serve national brands only. Tubi has local targeting but no interactive games and no attribution. The local SMB plus offline redemption intersection belongs entirely to Arenza.",
    "The revenue model quantifies the advantage. Based on 500,000 viewers and 4 million monthly impressions, the pure targeting status quo generates $181,800 per month. The combined Arenza model generates $258,000 to $295,000 per month: $220,000 from enriched programmatic CPM at $55 average, $30,000 to $60,000 from sponsor prediction activation fees, and $8,000 to $15,000 from commerce and coupon transaction fees. The combined model generates 42 to 62% higher revenue per channel while measurably improving the viewer experience and retention simultaneously.",
    "Phase two introduces the CMXS token layer as an expansion of the core engagement model. Viewers earn prediction points through gameplay, those points convert to CMXS tokens at a fixed exchange rate with no financial buy-in required. Within the CMXS ecosystem, tokens function as digital tools allowing users to stake for enhanced reward tiers, VIP physical experiences, or governance rights over platform features. The regulatory structure is deliberately modeled on the Helium HNT network, where tokens are awarded purely for service rendered, in this case attention plus data contribution.",
    "The engineering roadmap covers sprints seven through nine over weeks 13 to 18. Sprint seven builds the Prediction Engine Core: SCTE-35 trigger hooks, WebSocket resolution polling, and streak multiplier logic. Sprint eight delivers the Rewards Wallet and POS Integration: deep link commerce, APNs temporal push notification templates, and the CoreML profile enrichment bridge. Sprint nine adds Leaderboards and Scaling: social share mechanics, tvOS Focus Engine adaptation for Apple TV, and end-to-end POS integration testing across Toast, Square, and Clover.",
    "Three dichotomies define Arenza's long-term position. Commodity versus Moat: passive profile targeting is a commodity that any platform can replicate. Gamified prediction creates a defensible, proprietary content moat because it is the content itself. Attention versus Action: Arenza transforms second-screen attention drift into the highest-converting, most verifiable ad unit in broadcasting. Scale versus Precision: the platform bridges the massive scale of national Sports FAST distribution with the hyper-local precision of SMB retail point-of-sale attribution. The ultimate goal is that the platform becomes a habit, not a utility.",
]

